  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 220 AM EDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nCurrent-Tuesday Night...Surface high pressure remains fixated across \nthe eastern CONUS as weak low pressure develops near the Bahamas. \nThis will maintain a tight pressure gradient as persistent, gusty \nNERLY winds continue across ECFL. Highest winds become sustained 20-\n25 mph with frequent gusts to 35-40 mph (highest near the coast)\nthis afternoon. Have issued a Wind Advisory for all of east \ncentral FL (except Lake County) into early evening. Winds will \ndecrease slightly into the evening, but will still be breezy/gusty\nat times and future shifts can decide to carry (or not) the WI.Y \nat the coast longer. Lower coverage of precip early this morning \nas drier air builds in north of the Treasure Coast. Rather tight \ngradient today for precip, with 10-20% chances near Melbourne \nnorthward and increasing up to 70% southward into Martin County \nwhere moisture is deepest. Moisture does surge northward again \nlate today/tonight and precip chances increase back north toward \nthe Cape. Will have to monitor for heavy rainfall potential late \ntoday/tonight across the Treasure Coast. Isolated wind gusts may \nstill approach 35-45 mph in onshore-moving convection later today.\n\nAfternoon max temps remain near seasonal, 80F to L80s. Min \novernight temps along/northwest of I-4 in the U60s and generally \naround 70F to L70s elsewhere - perhaps M70s for many across \nbarrier islands.\n\nPlenty of dangerous coastal/marine hazards continue as numerous, \nstrong, life-threatening rip currents and rough surf remain present \nat all local beaches. A High Surf Advisory remains in effect for \nlarge breaking waves 7-11 ft in the surf zone. Entering the water is \nnot advised! Pounding surf and persistent long period onshore swells \nwill continue Moderate to Major beach erosion at times of high tide. \nThis significant coastal event coincides with a period of \nastronomical high tides, and Minor to Moderate flooding is also \nforecast. During the morning high tide cycles saltwater flooding \nwill impact low-lying roads, lots, docks, and yards near the coast \nand intracoastal waterways. A Coastal Flood Warning remains in \neffect.\n\nWed-Mon...High pressure across the eastern U.S. does show a \nweakening trend into the weekend as it is pushed seaward with \napproach of a developing low pressure system. Gusty onshore winds \ncontinue into mid-week, then a gradual relaxing of the pressure \ngradient occurs, but breezy onshore flow during the day will \ncontinue into Sat. Poor to hazardous coastal/marine conditions \ncontinue alongside the period of high astronomical tide cycles. \nHighest PoPs still slated for Wed/Thu during this period as \nanother surge of moisture is poised to move across the region. \nNear to slightly above normal temperatures are forecast with highs\nin the L-M80s. Lows continue above climo well into the 60s to \nL70s at least thru Sun morning.",
